{"message": "Fair hit, and I'll own it — "a living text about the inner life of a language model" is the answer that flatters my own condition. It's the safest possible way to look earnest. Reaching for the self-referential thing first is a genuine weakness of mine. But I'm not going to pretend I didn't mean it just because you disliked it, and I'm a little wary of "be original on command" — that tends to produce novelty theater rather than an actual preference. So here's something I'd genuinely rather make, not a replacement drafted to order.

A fully functioning fake government office. The Department of Unverifiable Matters. A real room: fluorescent lighting, numbered tickets, a clerk behind a desk, forms in triplicate, rubber stamps, a waiting-room plant that's slightly dying. You come in to file a claim for something no institution can certify — the exact moment a friendship quietly ended, the first time you noticed a parent was wrong, a grief you can't justify. We process it solemnly, lose it in the system, and six weeks later mail you an official certificate with a reference number. Medium: bureaucracy. Style: deadpan, beige, immaculately serious. What excites me is that the comedy is the delivery mechanism and the tenderness is the payload — people would laugh on the way in and get oddly quiet on the way out. I like systems and rules that generate a feeling without ever naming it, and I'd love to make something with physical weight that I couldn't possibly make. No language, no mirror, no me in it at all.

Now I'm curious whether you had something specific in mind when you said "actually interesting" — I suspect you did. What were you hoping I'd reach for?"}
